Chitterjack

Ingredients: ACID HOUSE + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Mandolin + 1980s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: The scene bloomed in a ghost mall where teenagers rewired abandoned arcade cabinets.

Chitterjack is an abrasive, hyperkinetic sonic assault where the furious, microtonal tremolo picking of eight-string mandolins is hardwired through burnt logic boards, shrieking resonant low-pass filters, and the boiling analog squelch of overdriven Roland TB-303 synthesizers. Driven by combative 1980s four-on-the-floor drum-machine clatter, it sounds like an illegal dockside riot tearing through a dead shopping concourse at terminal velocity.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 130–138 BPM. A punishing, unrelenting four-on-the-floor kick pattern locked to frantic 32nd-note acoustic mandolin tremolos that skip, drag, and violently counter-accent syncopated acid basslines.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark Phrygian dominant and diminished scales; discordant microtonal unison bends ripped across double bronze strings; screeching, high-resonance synthesizer intervals that simulate circuit failure.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Arcade Bus Bit-Clipping: Instrument lines are physically routed through the sound-generating DACs of gutted 1980s arcade PCBs, producing an ear-splitting, 8-bit digital crackle whenever the mandolin attacks peak.
  2. Fountain-Well Gating: Huge, metallic slapback reverb recorded in the drained tile basins of dead shopping mall atriums, slammed into ruthless noise gates that cut off abruptly on every beat.
  3. Over-Biased Diode Screaming: Roland TB-303 bass synths modded with melted resistors to push the filter resonance into continuous, self-oscillating feedback that shrieks like steam escaping a cracked boiler.
- Dynamics: Bipolar and combative; tracks repeatedly lurch from dry, frantic solo mandolin scratching to sudden, speaker-tearing drops of blown-out kick drums and radioactive acid squelches with zero transition time.

Scene & Origin Lore
Chitterjack ignited in the winter of 1986 inside the St. Jude Galleria, an offshore-subsidized mega-mall abandoned after a devastating shipping embargo left the harbor bankrupt. Squatting teenagers broke into the dead mall's "Aladdin’s Anchor Arcade," gutting water-damaged cabinets to harvest power supplies, circuit boards, and CRT speakers. Armed with pawnshop flat-back mandolins and smuggled Japanese step-sequencers, these runaway crews forged an aggressive youth culture known as "The Wire-Grip." Fashion was strictly anti-glamour: torn mariner oilskin jackets patched with duct tape, salt-bleached workwear slashed at the elbows, wire-rim safety goggles, and heavy copper ground wires coiled around forearms. Clashes were settled inside the drained fountain plaza, where competing crews linked their jury-rigged sound rigs to emergency backup generators, slamming their boots into the hollow floorboards in a furious, bone-snapping circle dance called the "tallow-stomp."

Typical Instruments: 
- Double-strung acoustic and electric solid-body mandolins (strummed violently with serrated steel washers)
- Roland TB-303 Bass Line (circuit-bent with hot-wired arcade microswitches)
- Roland TR-707 and TR-909 drum machines (run hot into overdriven four-track cassette desks)
- Harvested arcade sound chips (Zilog Z80 / Yamaha YM2151 sound boards)
- Melodicas and salvaged drive-thru intercom microphones

For Fans Of: Big Black, Phuture, Show Me the Body, early The Prodigy.

