Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Shoal Bells Bite the Transformer
- Story & Context: When seventeen-year-old Marlo Vance jammed a stripped eight-string mandolin jack straight into the cooling fan of an overturned Depth Charge arcade cabinet at the St. Jude Galleria, the scent of scalded dust and evaporated orange soda syrup hit the air like gunpowder. A wild power arc jumped from the coin slot to his copper wrist cuffs, throwing the entire upper mezzanine into sudden blackness while the cabinet's sound card squealed a hideous, four-on-the-floor square wave through the dead mall's intercom. Marlo did not drop the pick; he hammered the doubled brass strings until blood smeared the fretboard and screamed, "Spit the salt out, bridge the fuse!" right into the gaping ventilation shaft. Within twenty minutes, forty kids in torn offshore flotation vests had breached the plywood barricades, turning the shattered fountain basin into an open trench of stomping boots and screaming acid loops.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro cinematic shot of a glowing copper coil wrapping tightly around a vintage arcade game board, sparks flying across shattered mall tiles, overlaid with surreal glowing text: "Spit the salt out, bridge the fuse!" in scratched, glowing green phosphor vector graphics.

Track 2: "Who Cleared You for High Water?"
- Story & Context: Holed up inside the boarded-over Orange Julius stall, Corinne "Wharf-Rat" Pike scrubbed battery acid from her knuckles with a rag that reeked of damp fiberglass and sulfur. She had just sabotaged the arcade motherboard cluster powering the North Pier syndicate’s rig, slipping past patrol guard dogs by crawling through flooded utility culverts with three fried Z80 sound chips clutched between her teeth. As she wired the chips into her amplified flat-back mandolin to produce a jagged, coughing 134-BPM downbeat, her hands shook from the voltage leaks that stung her palms like jellyfish. She leaned over the shuddering cabinet frame and spat into the microphone, "I tore the clock out because your tide was running slow!" knowing the sirens circling the perimeter parking lot were already too late to choke the bass.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Harsh flash photography of a young woman crouched behind a rusted arcade cabinet in a dark retail stall, holding a smoking soldering iron and a scratched mandolin, her eyes wide with defiant rage, surrounded by tangled ribbon cables and peeling neon decals.

Track 3: Peak Resonance at Slack Tide
- Story & Context: By November of 1988, the Galleria was an echoing concrete reef where cold condensation dripped relentlessly from rusted ceiling girders onto thirty gutted Sea Wolf monitors glowing green in the dark. When municipal bailiffs finally cracked the padlocks with sledgehammers, they found no teenagers—only a phantom battery of three dozen mandolins suspended on clotheslines over auto-strumming box fans, their pickups soldered directly into an unholy daisy-chain of screaming, overdriven 303 synthesizers. The resulting sub-bass pressure had cracked the mall's load-bearing pillars and vibrated the stagnant fountain sludge into geometric standing waves, humming with a final looping tape broadcast that hissed: "The current doesn't drown, it digs!" It took municipal engineers three full weeks to cut the power cables, during which entire flocks of harbor gulls nested in the warm, roaring carcasses of the arcade cabinets.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric wide shot of a cavernous, abandoned 1980s shopping mall bathed in eerie emerald light from dozens of CRT monitors, floor cracked and flooded with murky water, acoustic mandolins hanging motionless like harpoons from the ceiling wires under a shattered skylight.

